  "about": "Boulders (boulders-v1, terrain-v92): rock lying on the land, made of the hardest geological unit within 64 blocks under each site's soil (hard rock outlasts its surroundings: quartzite and granite first, shale and soapstone last, loose sand and gravel never), without the near-surface climate tint, so one region's boulders share their rock. Each cellBlocks cell holds at most one candidate anywhere in the cell; a candidate closer than spacingBlocks to a higher-priority neighbour stands down, so footprints never overlap. A site's chance, kind, size and orientation come from seed and cell; its biome (the riven: biome its centre reports) gives the chance and the kind weights. Every kind is a heightfield: each column of a footprint holds rock from its ground block up to the site's ground plus the kind's height there, so a boulder never overhangs and a tree or the preview sees it as the column's ground. A site stands only on dry land (no water or sea in its footprint) whose columns lie within maximumSlopeBlocks of its centre. Kinds: dome (a rounded boulder or an erratic: an elliptical dome, roughened per column), tor (stacked slabs shrinking upward, each offset a little), spire (an outcrop rising to a rough point). radius and height are ranges in blocks; aspect is the short axis over the long; roughness is the per-column jitter in blocks.",
